From 330d4623218d6c629d6013ea13122b5442e6f5f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 02:11:48 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] lib_base: Build with mingw-w64

The Windows code assumes the MSVC toolchain and the full Windows SDK.

* Add base/platform/win/mingw_sdk, put on the include path for MinGW
  only, with stand-ins for SDK headers mingw-w64 lacks or ships in part:
  - wrl/implements.h, wrl/event.h, wrl/module.h: the classic COM part of
    WRL (RuntimeClass, Implements, FtmBase, Make, Callback and the
    out-of-process Module/CoCreatableClass registration used for toast
    activation). mingw-w64 only has the ComPtr side; its own wrl/module.h
    is included first.
  - UserConsentVerifierInterop.h: the Windows Hello window interop
    interface.
  - ShObjIdl_core.h: mingw-w64 keeps those interfaces in <shobjidl.h>.

* base_windows_winrt.cpp defines the WINRT_IMPL_* entry points of
  C++/WinRT to route them through dynamically resolved pointers (the
  Windows 7 baseline). With MSVC those names alias the real functions
  through /alternatename; with MinGW the declarations are __asm__ labels
  of the real function names, so the definitions clash with the import
  libraries. Leave them out and link runtimeobject instead.

* GUID_POWER_SAVING_STATUS is not declared by mingw-w64 yet; define it.

* GetProcAddress() returns a function pointer, which GCC does not
  convert to void* implicitly.

* The DLL search path is restricted to System32. A MinGW build uses the
  shared libraries of its environment, installed next to the executable,
  and Qt plugins loaded later (e.g. the WebP image format plugin needed
  for the emoji) pull more of them in by name. Keep the application
  directory searchable there, otherwise the emoji sprites fail to load
  and the app aborts on start.
